  "streakCelebrationFirst": "One day in!",
  "@streakCelebrationFirst": {
    "description": "The caption under the celebration's span ladder on the first day of a run. Checked before streakCelebrationChasing and deliberately outranks it: a run of 1 after a broken run is day one, because that is what the number above it says, so a reader coming back is told the same thing as a reader starting — the record does not hold a lapse against them. It replaced \"Day one. The rest is just tomorrow.\", which was improvised in the build rather than chosen: it appears in no spec, no plan and no mockup, and was the only string in this cluster with no description here. It read as condescending to a returning reader and its second clause did not resolve into a meaning. Note what the guard costs, which is open rather than overlooked: the ladder above reads `best`, not `streak`, so on a night where a lapsed reader has a record to chase this line is the only nearby text and it says nothing about that record, leaving the fill unexplained."
  },
  "streakSpanWeek": "1 week",
  "@streakSpanWeek": {
    "description": "A rung on the streak page's span ladder (StreakSpanTrack). Four spans, named as periods rather than counts: a row of ring badges reading 7 / 30 / 100 / 365 over Week / Month / 100 days / Year said the same thing twice and did not cohere, mixing three named periods with one raw count. 100 days has no shorter name, which is why it is the one rung still stated as a count."
  },
  "streakSpanMonth": "1 month",
  "streakSpanHundred": "100 days",
  "streakSpanYear": "1 year",
  "streakCelebrationChasing": "{days, plural, other{{days} more days to beat your best.}}",
  "@streakCelebrationChasing": {
    "description": "The caption under the celebration's progress bar while a run is short of the reader's own record. No =1 case, and it is unreachable rather than forgotten: the bar only shows while best > streak, so best >= streak + 1, so the distance (best + 1 - streak) is never below 2. Beating a record takes one more day than matching it. It replaced \"{days} more days to the {target}-day seal\", which named a reward that does not exist: kStreakMilestones fed a bar and that sentence and nothing else, there is no award path in lib/, and \"seal\" was already the Library Card's logo emboss, which every card carries regardless of any streak. longestStreakProvider ships, so a reader's own record is the one stake the app can actually point at.",
    "placeholders": {
      "days": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "streakCelebrationRecord": "Your longest run yet.",
  "@streakCelebrationRecord": {
    "description": "Shown instead of the countdown when the run is the longest on record. longestReadingRun counts the current run, so this is the case where streak == best \u2014 the reader is setting the record as they read, which is a real reward that costs no new feature."
  },
  "streakCelebrationGo": "Keep it going"
